<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">The Wealth of Nations</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1776">1776</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as An Inquiry into the Nature and Causes of the Wealth of Nations</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Wealth of Nations, in full An Inquiry into the Nature and Causes of the Wealth of Nations, is a book by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adam-smith">Adam Smith</a> published in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in 1776. It argued that wealth comes from labour and the division of labour, and that free competition, guided as if by an invisible hand, serves the public good better than mercantilist regulation. It is the founding work of classical economics.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/the-wealth-of-nations.jpg" alt="The Wealth of Nations" width="320" height="383" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gerhard Streminger · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wealth_of_Nations.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Wealth of Nations was published in two volumes on 9 March 1776, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scottish-enlightenment">Scottish Enlightenment</a>. It was the product of seventeen years of notes and earlier studies, and took Smith some ten years to write. It drew on the conversation of economists of the time, such as Nicholas Magens, about conditions at the beginning of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/industrial-revolution">Industrial Revolution</a>. In the 18th century much of the world economy was dominated by European feudal and mercantilist institutions, with hereditary privilege, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a>, colonial trading monopolies and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protective-tariff">protective tariffs</a>. Smith offered a reformed economic theory to replace the mercantilist and physiocratic theories.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>Book I holds that the division of labour caused a greater increase in production than any other factor, and that it arises from humans' propensity to barter and is limited by the extent of the market. Book II treats stock and capital, and Book III the different progress of opulence in different nations, in which Smith argues that capital is directed first to agriculture, then to manufactures and last to foreign commerce.</p><p>Book IV attacks <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercantilism">mercantilism</a>, in particular protectionist tariffs and the idea that large reserves of precious metals are necessary for a country's success. Smith rejected the view that wealth came from zero-sum competition between states. He argued that individuals pursuing their own interest often promote that of society, using the metaphor of an invisible hand, which appears only once in the book. He also criticised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>, rent-seeking by landlords, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/primogeniture">primogeniture</a>, public debt and military spending for imperial purposes, and favoured public education, a judiciary and a standing army. Book V sets out four maxims of taxation: proportionality, transparency, convenience and efficiency, and Smith called a land value tax most reasonable.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The first edition sold out in six months. The printer William Strahan wrote that Hume thought it required too much thought to be as popular as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-gibbon">Edward Gibbon</a>'s history, and Gibbon praised it as an extensive science in a single book. In 1791 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-madison">James Madison</a> cited it in Congress against a national bank, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-jefferson">Thomas Jefferson</a> called it the best book on money and commerce, unless Say's Political Economy could be had. It was translated into French, German and Spanish in the late 18th century, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-inquisition">Spanish Inquisition</a> banned the French translation.</p><p>The book did not at once transform policy, but it became one of the most influential of modern times. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Karl Marx</a> called it the true <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a> of the classical political economy of the 18th century, and Marxian economics developed as a critique of the tradition it established. Modern scholars stress that Smith used the invisible hand sparingly, acknowledged the dangers of monopolies and supported some state intervention.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “The Wealth of Nations” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Scottish Enlightenment flourished after the union: David Hume, Adam Smith, whose <b>Wealth of Nations</b> appeared in 1776, and Adam Ferguson shaped modern philosophy, economics and social science, while Samuel Johnson’s Dictionary (1755) and the novels of Daniel Defoe, Samuel Richardson and Henry Fielding helped establish modern English…</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-great-britain">Kingdom of Great Britain · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The French physiocrats called for free trade in grain, and Adam Smith, in <b>The Wealth of Nations</b> of 1776, argued that the division of labour and free exchange create wealth.</blockquote><a href="/movement/enlightenment">Enlightenment · Aims</a></li><li><blockquote>Adam Smith argued in <b>The Wealth of Nations</b> (1776) that Britain’s monopoly of colonial trade served merchants rather than the nation.</blockquote><a href="/government/colonial-rule">Colonial rule · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Adam Smith wrote of feudal government in <b>The Wealth of Nations</b> (1776), Enlightenment writers used the word to attack the privileges of their own day, and Karl Marx made feudalism the stage of history before capitalism, defined by lords’ control of land and their exploitation of the peasants…</blockquote><a href="/government/feudalism">Feudalism · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>In economics, Smith’s <b>The Wealth of Nations</b> (1776) argued that when prices and competition are left free of government regulation, the pursuit of self-interest maximises a society’s wealth, as if led by an “invisible hand”; government should be limited to defence, public works and justice.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/liberalism">Liberalism · Core Ideas</a></li><li><blockquote>The physiocrats of France were the first school to reject mercantilism completely, and Smith’s <b>The Wealth of Nations</b> (1776) rebutted it at length, often in simplified or exaggerated form: he saw it as a conspiracy of manufacturers and merchants against consumers, resting on “the popular folly of confusing…</blockquote><a href="/ideology/mercantilism">Mercantilism · Critiques &amp; Debates</a></li></ul></section>
